Our Rust control daemon binds `/run/ctld/rpc.sock`. Whenever an operator accidentally invokes `ctld` a second time while Instance 1 is already running, Instance 2 exits with `Address already in use` as expected — BUT Instance 1's `/run/ctld/rpc.sock` immediately vanishes from `/run/ctld/`, orphaning the live daemon! Why did Instance 2's failed startup delete Instance 1's socket?

`src/socket_guard.rs`
```rust
use std::fs;
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

pub struct SocketCleanupGuard {
    pub path: PathBuf,
}

impl SocketCleanupGuard {
    pub fn new(path: &Path) -> Self {
        Self {
            path: path.to_path_buf(),
        }
    }
}

impl Drop for SocketCleanupGuard {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        let _ = fs::remove_file(&self.path);
    }
}
```

`src/server.rs`
```rust
use std::io;
use std::os::unix::net::UnixListener;
use std::path::Path;
use crate::socket_guard::SocketCleanupGuard;

pub struct RpcServer {
    _guard: SocketCleanupGuard,
    listener: UnixListener,
}

impl RpcServer {
    pub fn bind(sock_path: &Path) -> io::Result<Self> {
        let guard = SocketCleanupGuard::new(sock_path);
        let listener = UnixListener::bind(sock_path)?;
        Ok(Self {
            _guard: guard,
            listener,
        })
    }

    pub fn accept_loop(&self) -> io::Result<()> {
        for conn in self.listener.incoming() {
            let _stream = conn?;
        }
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

`src/main.rs`
```rust
mod server;
mod socket_guard;

use std::path::Path;

fn main() -> std::io::Result<()> {
    let srv = server::RpcServer::bind(Path::new("/run/ctld/rpc.sock"))?;
    srv.accept_loop()
}
```

